                    <occupation>publisher</occupation>
                    <occupation>bookseller</occupation>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>John Debrett was a publisher and printer.</p>
                        <p>The first mentions in the diary are in a series of calls, alongside <persName key="ROB01">George Robinson</persName> and <persName key="PER01">James Perry</persName>, in Nov 1795. 
  
 After that all references are to his premises, 36 in total, 1795-7. According to the <title>DNB</title>, Debrett set up shop at John Almon's former business at 178 Piccadilly,
      London, in 1791, and hence inherited some of Almon's Whig patrons.  The shop also became a fashionable hub for literary and political conversation.  
    The Identification has been confirmed through matching dates and the long lists of 
  people whom Godwin recorded meeting at Debrett's.</p>
                    </note>
                    <bibl type="other">
                        <title>DNB</title>
                    </bibl>
                </person>
